__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
.pytest_cache/
.mypy_cache/
*.egg-info/
.DS_Store

# Coverage's own database. Written into the working tree by a measured run, and
# not ignored until now — so `git add .` would have committed a 143 KB binary
# artefact of one machine's run. `tests/audit.py` walks untracked files that are
# not ignored, precisely so that a stray like this is visible.
.coverage
.coverage.*

# Deformed assets written by the example. `--out` can point anywhere, so this is
# by extension rather than by directory.
*.ply

# ── tracked by exception: the sample asset ──────────────────────────────────
# `data/penguin_original.ply` is 5.6 MB and the penguin example cannot run
# without it. GitHub's per-file limit is 100 MB, so tracking it is simply fine —
# and it is the difference between a bare clone that runs the example and one
# that only reads about it, which is the whole point of this repository. Git LFS
# and a fetch script were both considered, as workarounds for a size constraint
# that turns out not to exist.
!data/penguin_original.ply

# ── and by exception: the deformed asset the §6.4 figure is drawn from ──────
# `out/penguin_deformed.ply` is an OUTPUT, written by `examples/run_penguin.py` —
# which is why it sits in `out/` beside the published table rather than in `data/`,
# where the port record requires a provenance row for every file and it would have
# to claim to have been copied from somewhere it was not.
#
# It is committed because the alternative is a 19-second ARAP solve in the test
# path, and that solve is the one genuinely BLAS-sensitive step in this repository:
# the solver diagnostic already found 20 of 40 values moving between backends on
# identical numpy and scipy. Committing the geometry puts the figure's inputs on
# the same footing as the table's — fixed bytes, re-derivable arithmetic — instead
# of making a published figure depend on which BLAS an examiner happens to have.
!out/penguin_deformed.ply

# ── and nothing under evidence/ is ever ignored ─────────────────────────────
# Evidence is copied in and pinned by sha256; none of it is generated here, so an
# extension-based rule has no business matching any of it. This negation is not
# hypothetical tidiness: a blanket `*.png` briefly lived above and shadowed the
# six vendored characterisation figures. They survived only because they had been
# committed before the rule was added — a fresh `git add` would have dropped
# them, and the repository would have cloned without the figures Chapter 6 cites
# while every test still passed. `tests/test_suite_shape.py` now asserts this.
!evidence/**


